<p class="mt-indent-1 lt-chem-157675">With 1.5 mg Cu/L giving an absorbance of 0.20, the emission line at 324.8 nm has the best sensitivity. In addition, it is the most intense emission line, which decreases the uncertainty in the measured absorbance.</p>

<section class="box-example"><header><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Analytical_Chemistry_2.1_(Harvey)/10:_Spectroscopic_Methods/10.04:_Atomic_Absorption_Spectroscopy" class="mt-section"><span id="Example_10.4.1"></span><h5 id="Example_10.4.1-157675"><span class="lt-icon-default">Example 10.4.1
</span></h5></div></header>

<p class="lt-chem-157675">To evaluate the method described in Representative Method 10.4.1, a series of external standard is prepared and analyzed, providing the results shown here [Crawford, A. J.; Bhattacharya, S. K. &ldquo;Microanalysis of Copper and Zinc in Biopsy-Sized Tissue Specimens by Atomic Absorption Spectroscopy Using a Stoichiometric Air-Acetylene Flame,&rdquo; Varian Instruments at Work, Number AA&ndash;46, April 1985].</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th class="mt-align-center" scope="col">&micro;g Cu/mL</th>
            <th class="mt-align-center" scope="col">absorbance</th>
            <th class="mt-align-center" scope="col">&micro;g Cu/mL</th>
